From d9cd44e9b7f3c0f96dd457d5afa8ac903afb3490 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:27:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Add an experimental backend="hegel" provider and
 engine-equivalence tests

settings(backend="hegel") routes every generate-phase draw to libhegel while
Hypothesis keeps the run loop, database, health checks and shrinking (it
shrinks by replaying the recorded choices through its own provider, as for
any alternative backend). libhegel runs with only its generate phase, a
large test_cases budget, no database and all health checks suppressed.

Draws map one-to-one onto libhegel primitives except where the engines'
vocabularies differ, each documented on the provider:

- unbounded / half-bounded integers are clamped the way HypothesisProvider
  clamps them; weights are emulated (keys picked uniformly);
- libhegel needs allow_infinity spelled out and refuses NaN alongside a
  finite bound, so bounded-with-NaN draws never produce NaN;
- alphabets expressible as a codepoint range plus a small include / exclude
  list become a libhegel text generator; others fall back to libhegel's
  collection sizing plus HypothesisProvider's character index distribution;
- constant injection stays in Python (its pool is mined from the user's
  source), using a private PRNG.

The tests check the provider contract (every draw permitted by its
constraints), the whole pipeline (generation, shrinking to the same minimal
examples as the Python engine), and replay semantics: a Hypothesis choice
sequence encoded as a libhegel reproduce blob replays through libhegel value
for value, the contract that would make databases and blobs portable.

